<GuidePage title='Suspense & Streaming - App Router Guide' root='..'>
  <div class='kicker'>Chapter 11 · Part B: Contain the Unknown</div>
  <h1>Suspense &amp; Streaming</h1>
  <p class='lede'>
    Loading is not a component concern. It's a structural one. Suspense moves "what shows while the
    page waits" out of components and into the tree, where the framework can stream around it. This
    chapter covers how suspension works, what triggers it, and the loading-state doctrine that
    follows from it.
  </p>

  <h2 id='mechanism'>11.1 How Suspense actually works</h2>
  <p>
    A <code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code> boundary is a declaration about the tree:
    <em>"everything below this point may not be ready yet; here is what to show in its place."</em>
    When a component inside the boundary <strong>suspends</strong> (it's waiting on something asynchronous),
    React walks <em>up</em> the tree to the nearest boundary, renders its <code>fallback</code> there,
    and, when the wait resolves, swaps the real content into the exact same slot. The component itself
    never knows any of this happened. That's the entire point: the waiting is handled <em>above</em> the
    component, by structure, not <em>inside</em> it, by state.
  </p>

