  <h3 id='under-the-hood'>Under the hood: rendering is speculative, committing is atomic</h3>
  <p>
    It's worth slowing down on what "suspends" mechanically means, because the mental model most of
    React's guarantees hang on lives here. During render, a component that isn't ready
    <strong>throws</strong>: historically a promise, today the thenable protocol behind
    <code>use()</code> and the framework's data APIs. React catches it at the nearest boundary. And here
    is the part that matters: that render was <strong>an attempt, not a commitment</strong>. The
    suspended component was never mounted. No DOM was created for it, none of its effects ran, no
    lifecycle happened, nothing it might have rendered reaches the screen. React discards the
    attempt, commits the boundary's fallback instead, and when the thenable resolves, <strong
      >re-renders the subtree from scratch</strong
    >. If the retry completes, the finished result is swapped in whole.
    <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='critical' />
  </p>
  <p>
    That is the two-phase design underneath all of it: <strong>rendering</strong> is speculative work
    in memory (interruptible, discardable, retryable), while <strong>committing</strong> is atomic (all
    or nothing, and the only step that touches the DOM or fires effects). "Mounted" means <em
      >committed</em
    >, not rendered. This is why a component can never observe its own suspension, why no effect
    ever fires for an attempt that suspended, and why the user never sees a half-finished tree:
    half-finished trees exist only in memory, and memory is cheap to throw away.
  </p>
  <p>
    The React team's own explanation of this, from the talk that introduced Suspense, is
    <strong>version control</strong>. The UI on screen is <code>main</code>: committed, stable,
    interactive. When React renders the next UI, it works on a <em>branch</em>: if something
    suspends, the branch simply stays unmerged while the data loads, and <code>main</code> is untouched.
    When the branch finally completes, it merges: one atomic commit, never a half-finished merge. And
    note which git operation maps to which React phase: the <em>branch work</em> is rendering (speculative,
    in memory, free to take as long as the data takes), and the
    <em>merge</em> is the commit. Because the commit is flat, the new UI appears all at once: not a header,
    then rows, then a footer trickling onto the screen, but one instantaneous swap of finished work.
  </p>

