    <Fragment slot='caption'>
      The analogy is the React team's own, from the talk that introduced Suspense:
      <a href='https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nLF0n9SACd4&t=1740s' target='_blank' rel='noopener'
        >Beyond React 16 (JSConf Iceland, 2018)</a
      >, a little after the 29-minute mark, and it repays watching in full. Committed UI is <code
        >main</code
      >; a render attempt is an unmerged branch that can wait on data without touching the screen;
      resolution is two distinct moments: first the branch <em>completes</em> (a finished tree exists
      in memory, and HEAD points at what the screen should become), then the merge flattens it into <code
        >main</code
      > in one atomic commit. One footnote the analogy needs: when there is no old screen worth keeping
      (a first load), the boundary commits its <em>fallback</em> to
      <code>main</code> while the branch works, which is the streaming story of
      <a href='#streaming'>§11.2</a>; on navigations, transitions (<a href='#transitions'>§11.7</a>)
      are what keep the old <code>main</code> checked out instead.
    </Fragment>
