  <h3 id='triggers'>What triggers a Suspense boundary</h3>
  <p>
    These are framework mechanics, worth knowing precisely:
    <Chip k='critical' />
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>An async server component awaiting</strong> inside the boundary: the canonical App Router
      case. The server streams the fallback, keeps working, and streams the content when the await resolves.
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code'
          is:raw><span class="tok-com">{/* The await inside ReportRows suspends this boundary. */}</span>
&lt;<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span> <span class="tok-prop">fallback</span>={&lt;<span class="tok-fn">RowsSkeleton</span> /&gt;}&gt;
  &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ReportRows</span> <span class="tok-prop">programId</span>={id} /&gt;
&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span>&gt;

<span class="tok-kw">async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ReportRows</span>({ programId }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> rows = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">getRows</span>(programId);
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;<span class="tok-fn">TableBody</span> <span class="tok-prop">rows</span>={rows} /&gt;;
}</pre>
      </Snippet>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>use(promise)</code></strong> in a client component: React 19's way to read a promise
      (often one passed down from a server component) with Suspense semantics.
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code'
          is:raw><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">function</span> <span class="tok-fn">Comments</span>({ commentsPromise }) {
  <span class="tok-com">/* Suspends until the promise (started on the server) resolves. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> comments = <span class="tok-fn">use</span>(commentsPromise);
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;<span class="tok-fn">CommentList</span> <span class="tok-prop">comments</span>={comments} /&gt;;
}</pre>
      </Snippet>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Suspense-enabled data hooks</strong>: SWR with
      <code>suspense:&nbsp;true</code>, React Query's <code>useSuspenseQuery</code>.
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code'
          is:raw><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramStats</span>({ programId }) {
  <span class="tok-com">/* No isLoading flag: the hook suspends, the boundary shows the fallback. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { data } = <span class="tok-fn">useSWR</span>([<span class="tok-str">"stats"</span>, programId], fetcher, { <span class="tok-prop">suspense</span>: <span class="tok-kw">true</span> });
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;<span class="tok-fn">StatsRow</span> <span class="tok-prop">stats</span>={data} /&gt;;
}</pre>
      </Snippet>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>lazy()</code> components</strong> and dynamic imports while their code loads (<a
        href='07-first-render-js.html'>§7</a
      >).
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code'
          is:raw><span class="tok-kw">const</span> EnrollmentChart = <span class="tok-fn">dynamic</span>(() =&gt; <span class="tok-kw">import</span>(<span class="tok-str">"./EnrollmentChart"</span>));

<span class="tok-com">{/* While the chunk downloads, the boundary shows the fallback. */}</span>
&lt;<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span> <span class="tok-prop">fallback</span>={&lt;<span class="tok-fn">ChartSkeleton</span> /&gt;}&gt;
  &lt;<span class="tok-fn">EnrollmentChart</span> /&gt;
&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span>&gt;</pre>
      </Snippet>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>useSearchParams()</code></strong> in a client component requires a boundary above
      it during prerendering: a mandatory, mechanical case with its own dedicated pattern in
      <a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#searchparams-boundary'>§5.6</a>.
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code'
          is:raw><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">function</span> <span class="tok-fn">SearchInputInner</span>() {
  <span class="tok-com">/* Must render under a Suspense boundary; §5.6 bakes it into the component. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> params = <span class="tok-fn">useSearchParams</span>();
  <span class="tok-com">// …</span>
}</pre>
      </Snippet>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <Callout kind='info' title='Suspense is not a server-only feature'>
    <p>
      Notice that three of those five triggers fire in the browser. It is a common blind spot to
      think of Suspense as a server-rendering mechanism: a client component that reads a promise
      with <code>use()</code>, a Suspense-enabled data hook, or a
      <code>lazy()</code> chunk all suspend and reveal through the same boundary. The boundary doesn't
      care which side of the network its child runs on; it cares only that the child is waiting. So the
      whole doctrine of this chapter, that the boundary owns the loading state and the component never
      rolls its own, applies to genuinely-client subtrees exactly as it does to server data.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    Just as important is what does <em>not</em> trigger suspension:
    <strong>a fetch inside <code>useEffect</code> is invisible to React.</strong> Nothing suspends, no
    boundary activates, no streaming happens; the framework doesn't even know the component is waiting.
    Every hand-rolled <code>isLoading</code> flag in a codebase exists because the fetching pattern opted
    out of the mechanism that would have made it unnecessary.
  </p>
  <Callout kind='info' title="Boundaries don't re-trigger on updates">
    <p>
      A boundary that has already revealed content will <strong>not</strong> revert to its fallback when
      an update inside it suspends during a <em>transition</em>: router navigations and <code
        >startTransition</code
      > updates keep the existing content visible while the new content prepares. React 19.2 further batches
      how server-streamed boundaries reveal. The fallback is a <em>first-render</em> device; everything
      after first render should follow the stale-content rules in <a href='#skeletons'>§11.4</a>.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id='streaming'>11.2 Streaming: Suspense on the server</h2>
  <p>
    Without Suspense, server rendering is all-or-nothing: the response can't start until every fetch
    on the page finishes, so the slowest request sets the time-to-first-byte for the entire page.
    With Suspense boundaries, the server streams the page in order of readiness: the shell (layout,
    containers, everything known) goes out immediately, and each suspended region follows as its
    data resolves, injected into its reserved slot without re-rendering anything around it. (Making
    the waits themselves small and parallel, rather than just streaming around them, is
    <a href='13-blocking-requests.html'>§13</a>'s subject.)
  </p>

