      Recreated from the Next.js docs' streaming illustration: same fetches, same durations. Both
      lanes have a "first paint"; the difference is what it contains and when it happens. Blocking
      SSR paints once, the whole page, after the slowest fetch (TTFB and FCP both land at the far
      right). Streaming paints the shell and fallbacks immediately (TTFB and FCP at the far left),
      then each region follows the same pipeline: rendered on the server when its fetch resolves,
      streamed to the browser as a chunk, and swapped into its reserved slot, the fast header first
      (with the rows skeleton still showing), the slow table when it's ready. TTI is deliberately
      not marked: it depends on hydration, and <a href='04-ssr.html#tti'>§4.3</a> explains why it stopped
