  <p>
    Two placement rules follow: <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='critical' />
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong><code>loading.tsx</code> is a route-level Suspense boundary</strong> the framework wires
      up for you: it wraps the page, shows instantly on navigation, and is even <em>prefetched</em>
      along with the route so the fallback can appear before the server responds at all. Use it for the
      route transition, but treat it as the coarsest tool: a whole-page skeleton is exactly the page-level
      skeleton to avoid (<a href='#skeletons'>§11.4</a>). Prefer a
      <code>loading.tsx</code> that renders the page's known scaffold, with granular boundaries inside
      the page doing the real work.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Granular boundaries around each data-dependent region</strong>: one per self-fetching
      component (<a href='14-minimize-the-wires.html#streaming'>§14.3</a>), placed inside the known
      container (<a href='10-containers-content-shifting.html'>§10</a>). This is also what Partial
      Prerendering builds on: with
      <code>cacheComponents</code>, everything outside your Suspense boundaries becomes the static
      shell served instantly, and the boundaries define exactly where dynamic content streams in.
    </li>
  </ul>

