  <h3 id='wire'>What actually travels down the wire</h3>
  <p>
    Two streams cooperate during an initial load, and knowing them makes the rest of this section
    mechanical rather than magical. The <strong>HTML stream</strong> carries the page the user sees: the
    <strong>static shell</strong> (layouts, navigation, containers, every Suspense fallback) goes out
    in the first chunk, and as each suspended server component resolves, React streams its finished HTML
    in a hidden node along with a tiny inline script that <strong
      >swaps it into the fallback's slot immediately</strong
    >, before the page's JavaScript bundle has loaded and without waiting for hydration. Alongside
    it rides the <strong>component payload</strong> (<a href='04-ssr.html#routing'>§4.5</a>'s RSC
    payload), the serialized tree React later hydrates from; on client-side navigations,
    <em>only</em> the payload travels, no HTML at all. One practical consequence is worth having seen
    once: in DevTools, a streaming page shows an early time-to-first-byte followed by a long content-download
    phase, and the raw response is literally the shell, then <code>&lt;div hidden&gt;</code> chunks arriving
    one boundary at a time. If a page you expected to stream arrives as one late lump instead, suspect
    the pipes: reverse proxies (nginx buffers by default), CDNs, serverless platforms without response
    streaming, and even compression layers can buffer the chunks back into a single delayed response.
  </p>

