  <h3 id='pass-promises'>Keep the shell static: pass promises, not awaits</h3>
  <p>
    The size of that instantly-sent shell is decided by one habit.
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='critical' /> Every request-time access (<code>params</code>, <code>searchParams</code>,
    <code>cookies()</code>, <code>headers()</code>, a data fetch) that you <code>await</code> at the top
    of a layout or page makes <em>everything below that point</em> dynamic: it all leaves the shell and
    waits with you. The fix is the same push-down instinct as
    <a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#push-down'>§5.3</a>, applied to awaits:
    <strong
      >start the work where it's convenient, but resolve it only inside the boundary that needs it</strong
    >. These APIs return promises precisely so the promise can travel.
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">app/(program)/layout.tsx - start the work, don't await it</span><span class="tok-kw">import</span> { cookies } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"next/headers"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export default function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramLayout</span>({ children }: { children: <span class="tok-fn">ReactNode</span> }) {
  <span class="tok-com">/* Not awaited: the layout, nav, and children all stay in the static
     shell. Only UserMenu suspends, inside its own boundary. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> cookieStore = <span class="tok-fn">cookies</span>();
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;div <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"frame"</span>&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Nav</span>&gt;
        &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span> <span class="tok-prop">fallback</span>={&lt;<span class="tok-fn">UserMenuSkeleton</span> /&gt;}&gt;
          &lt;<span class="tok-fn">UserMenu</span> <span class="tok-prop">cookiePromise</span>={cookieStore} /&gt;
        &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span>&gt;
      &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Nav</span>&gt;
      {children}
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      One <code>await cookies()</code> at the top of this layout would have pulled the entire route, children
      included, out of the static shell. Passing the promise down confines the dynamic access to the one
      component that genuinely needs it; the frame prerenders and ships instantly.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">app/programs/[programId]/page.tsx - unwrap the promise inline, inside the boundary</span><span class="tok-kw">export default function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramPage</span>({ params }: { params: <span class="tok-fn">Promise</span>&lt;{ programId: <span class="tok-kw">string</span> }&gt; }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramHero</span> /&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span> <span class="tok-prop">fallback</span>={&lt;<span class="tok-fn">StatsSkeleton</span> /&gt;}&gt;
        {params.<span class="tok-fn">then</span>(({ programId }) =&gt; (
          &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramStats</span> <span class="tok-prop">programId</span>={programId} /&gt;
        ))}
      &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span>&gt;
    &lt;/&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      The page never awaits <code>params</code>, so the hero paints in the shell; the inline <code
        >.then()</code
      > defers the access to inside the boundary while keeping <code>ProgramStats</code> simple: it receives
      a plain string, not a promise.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <h3 id='promises-as-props'>Streaming across the boundary: promises as props</h3>
  <p>
    The same trick crosses into client components. A server component can <strong>start</strong> a fetch
    and hand the unresolved promise to a client component as a prop; the promise can pass through as many
    layers as needed, and only the component that finally reads it with <code>use()</code> (<a
      href='19-new-primitives.html'>§19</a
    >) needs a Suspense boundary above it. This is the right shape when the <em>rendering</em> genuinely
    needs the client (a chart library, a canvas) but the <em>data</em> has no business being fetched from
    the browser:
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">app/reports/page.tsx - the fetch starts on the server; the client only reads it</span><span class="tok-kw">export default function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ReportsPage</span>() {
  <span class="tok-com">/* Started during server render, not awaited: the page doesn't block. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> statsPromise = <span class="tok-fn">getStats</span>();
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span> <span class="tok-prop">fallback</span>={&lt;<span class="tok-fn">ChartSkeleton</span> /&gt;}&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">StatsChart</span> <span class="tok-prop">dataPromise</span>={statsPromise} /&gt;
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
  </Snippet>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">StatsChart.tsx - a client leaf that suspends on a server-started fetch</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> { use } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"react"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">StatsChart</span>({ dataPromise }: { dataPromise: <span class="tok-fn">Promise</span>&lt;<span class="tok-fn">Stats</span>&gt; }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> stats = <span class="tok-fn">use</span>(dataPromise);
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Chart</span> <span class="tok-prop">data</span>={stats} /&gt;;
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      The fallback ships with the shell; when the server-side fetch resolves, the finished result
      streams in and the chart hydrates with its data already present. No client fetch, no client
      loading state, no waterfall. When several components across a subtree need the same promise,
      hand it to a context provider once and let each of them <code>use()</code> it from context.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <h3 id='progressive-reveal'>Progressive reveal with nested boundaries</h3>
  <p>
    Sibling boundaries resolve independently; <strong>nested</strong> boundaries resolve in layers, and
    that ordering is a design tool. Put the reviews boundary <em>inside</em> the product-details boundary
    and the page reveals itself outside-in: details first, reviews after, never reviews-before-details
    no matter which fetch wins the race. Use nesting when one region's content is meaningless without
    another's (a comments list before the post it belongs to reads as broken); use siblings (<a
      href='13-blocking-requests.html'>§13</a
    >) everywhere else, so independent regions never wait on each other.
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">nesting as an ordering tool - details always reveal before notes</span>&lt;<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span> <span class="tok-prop">fallback</span>={&lt;<span class="tok-fn">DetailsSkeleton</span> /&gt;}&gt;
  &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramDetails</span> <span class="tok-prop">programId</span>={programId} /&gt;
  <span class="tok-com">{/* Nested: this boundary isn't even visible until details resolve. */}</span>
  &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span> <span class="tok-prop">fallback</span>={&lt;<span class="tok-fn">NotesSkeleton</span> /&gt;}&gt;
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramNotes</span> <span class="tok-prop">programId</span>={programId} /&gt;
  &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span>&gt;
&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span>&gt;</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      If the notes resolve first, they wait: the outer boundary holds both until the details are in,
      then the inner boundary takes over for the notes alone. The reveal order is guaranteed by
      structure, not by racing fetches.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <h3 id='http-contract'>The HTTP contract: the status code ships with the first chunk</h3>
  <p>
    Streaming has one hard constraint worth internalizing.
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='very' /> The moment the first chunk is sent, the response's status code and headers are committed:
    the server said <code>200 OK</code> and cannot take it back. A <code>notFound()</code>
    that fires mid-stream can't become a real 404 (Next injects a <code>noindex</code> meta tag into the
    stream instead), and a <code>redirect()</code> mid-stream degrades to a client-side redirect rather
    than an HTTP one. The practical rule: anything that should produce a <em>real</em> status code (a
    404 for a record that doesn't exist, an auth redirect) belongs <strong
      >before the first boundary</strong
    >, as a fast check ahead of the slow work:
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">app/programs/[programId]/page.tsx - real 404s happen before the stream starts</span><span class="tok-kw">export default async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramPage</span>({ params }: <span class="tok-fn">Props</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { programId } = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> params;

  <span class="tok-com">/* A fast existence check, before any Suspense boundary: this can
     still be a real HTTP 404. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">if</span> (!(<span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">programExists</span>(programId))) <span class="tok-fn">notFound</span>();

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span> <span class="tok-prop">fallback</span>={&lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramSkeleton</span> /&gt;}&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramContent</span> <span class="tok-prop">programId</span>={programId} /&gt;
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      The cheap question (does this exist?) is answered before streaming begins, so crawlers and
      caches see an honest status code; the expensive question (what's in it?) streams behind the
      boundary. Errors thrown <em>after</em> streaming starts are still contained: the nearest <code
        >error.tsx</code
      > renders in place of the failed region and the rest of the page stands.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <h3 id='streaming-vitals'>Streaming and the vitals</h3>
  <p>
    The vitals story rounds this out, because boundary placement moves real numbers. TTFB and FCP
    decouple from data (the <a href='#streaming'>lane diagram above</a>); CLS is
    <a href='10-containers-content-shifting.html'>§10</a>'s territory (fallback and content share
    one honest footprint). Two more deserve a sentence each. <strong>LCP:</strong> if the page's largest
    element (a hero, the main heading) sits inside a Suspense boundary, it cannot paint until that boundary
    resolves; keep LCP elements in the shell, above the boundaries, and preload LCP images so the browser
    starts fetching them from the first chunk. <strong>INP:</strong> each boundary is also a <em
      >hydration unit</em
    >. React hydrates streamed regions independently and prioritizes whatever the user is touching,
    so granular boundaries break hydration into small tasks that keep the main thread responsive,
    where a boundary-less page hydrates as one long blocking pass. And the shell's first chunk
    carries the page's <code>&lt;link&gt;</code> and
    <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tags, so CSS, fonts, and JS start downloading during server think time
    instead of after it: streaming buys back the network idle that blocking rendering wastes.
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">the LCP element lives in the shell, above the boundaries</span>&lt;main&gt;
  <span class="tok-com">{/* Preloaded from the first chunk; painted with the shell. */}</span>
  &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Image</span> <span class="tok-prop">src</span>={hero} <span class="tok-prop">preload</span> <span class="tok-prop">alt</span>=<span class="tok-str">"Program cover"</span> /&gt;
  &lt;h1&gt;Program overview&lt;/h1&gt;

  <span class="tok-com">{/* Only the data-dependent region streams. */}</span>
  &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span> <span class="tok-prop">fallback</span>={&lt;<span class="tok-fn">StatsSkeleton</span> /&gt;}&gt;
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramStats</span> <span class="tok-prop">programId</span>={programId} /&gt;
  &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span>&gt;
&lt;/main&gt;</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      The hero image and heading are the page's largest paint, so they sit in the shell where the
      first chunk delivers them; <code>preload</code> puts a <code>&lt;link rel="preload"&gt;</code>
      in the head so the browser starts fetching the image before its tag even streams by. Wrap the same
      hero in the stats' boundary and LCP would inherit the stats query's latency for no reason.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <h3 id='fallback-trade'>Every boundary is a decision: an unblocked paint, for a fallback</h3>
  <p>
    A Suspense boundary is not free structure; it is a trade, and it helps to name both sides of it.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>What you buy:</strong> the page stops waiting for that region's data. The server ships the
      shell the moment it is ready, the browser paints it, and the slow region streams into its slot later;
      without the boundary, that one fetch holds the entire response hostage (the <a
        href='#streaming'>first diagram above</a
      >).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>What you pay:</strong> something must now occupy that region's space in the meantime. A
      boundary forces a fallback into existence, and the fallback is real UI: it has to reserve honest
      dimensions (<a href='10-containers-content-shifting.html'>§10</a>), look intentional next to
      finished content, and hand off to the real thing in a seamless swap.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    That makes "should this suspend?" a per-region judgment call, not a rule you can apply
    mechanically. Most of the time the answer is yes: suspend and stream, because one slow fetch
    must not set the time-to-first-paint for content that was ready. But the trade tilts the other
    way when a good fallback cannot be authored. If the region's height is genuinely unknowable in
    advance, the swap will shift content no matter how careful the skeleton is; if the data resolves
    in a handful of milliseconds, the fallback is a flash of noise that was never worth painting. In
    those cases it is legitimate to let the server block on the fetch, provided the wait is kept
    small and parallel (<a href='13-blocking-requests.html'>§13</a>).
  </p>
  <Callout kind='voice' title='The two questions to ask per region'>
    <p>
      First: is this data slow enough, often enough, that the rest of the page should not wait for
      it? If no, block and render it finished. If yes: can I draw a fallback that reserves this
      region's true shape? If yes, suspend. If no, fix the container until the answer is yes (<a
        href='10-containers-content-shifting.html'>§10</a
      >); shipping the shift is not one of the options.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id='no-own-loading'>11.3 Components must not define their own loading states</h2>
  <p>
    <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='critical' /> A component that owns its own loading indicator, whether that's a spinner, a
    skeleton, or any other placeholder, has made three decisions it has no business making: <em
      >that</em
    > it is worth waiting for (its parent's composition decides that), <em>where</em> the waiting UI appears
    (the boundary above decides that), and
    <em>what the rest of the page does meanwhile</em> (it can't know). The costs show up immediately:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Loading becomes un-composable.</strong> Compose three self-spinning components and you get
      three spinners resolving at different moments: spinner cascades, each swap shifting layout. With
      boundaries, the composing parent decides: one boundary around all three, or one each, without touching
      any of them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The loading UI is welded to the data mechanism.</strong> Move the fetch from client to server,
      add a cache, wrap it in a transition, and the hand-rolled
      <code>isLoading</code> plumbing has to be rewritten every time. A fallback on a boundary survives
      all of those changes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It's invisible to the framework.</strong> Streaming, PPR, and transition semantics all operate
      on boundaries. A component-owned spinner opts out of every one of them.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <Compare>
    <CompareCol kind='bad' label='✕ Component owns its loading state'>
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-bad'
          is:raw><span class="filename">ProgramStats.tsx</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramStats</span>({ programId }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { data, isLoading } = <span class="tok-fn">useProgram</span>(programId);

  <span class="tok-com">/* This component just decided that the page
     shows a spinner here. Nobody composing it
     gets a say. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">if</span> (isLoading) <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Spinner</span> /&gt;;
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;<span class="tok-fn">StatsRow</span> <span class="tok-prop">stats</span>={data.stats} /&gt;;
}</pre>
