  <h2 id='skeletons'>11.4 Skeletons: scoped, colocated, structural</h2>
  <p>
    The container doctrine is <a href='10-containers-content-shifting.html'>§10</a>'s subject:
    containers known and immediate, fallback and content swapped inside them, one shared footprint,
    and neither occupant bringing its own chrome. All of it applies verbatim to fallbacks:
    <strong>the fallback is simply the container's first occupant.</strong>
    <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='critical' /> What belongs to this chapter is the fallback itself, the skeleton:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong
        >Scope skeletons per server component (or per client component when needed), never per page.</strong
      >
      <Chip k='very' /> A page-level skeleton is a second, shadow implementation of the entire page layout.
      It drifts the moment anyone changes the real page, and nobody remembers to update it; the maintainability
      cost compounds with every route. A per-component skeleton is small, sits next to the component it
      mirrors (<code>StatsRow.tsx</code> / <code>StatsSkeleton.tsx</code>), and changes in the same
      PR when the component changes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Keep skeletons structural, not photorealistic.</strong> They exist to hold reserved space
      and signal "coming": a few bars matching the content's rough geometry. The more detailed the skeleton,
      the faster it drifts.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Skeletons appear exactly once per region: on first load.</strong> Never on refetch; that's
      the next section.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A skeleton stands in for the unknown, and only the unknown.</strong>
      <Chip k='critical' /> If a region contains anything knowable on first render (form inputs, buttons,
      headings, filter controls: everything that does not depend on the data), that content belongs on
      screen from the first paint, not buried under a region-wide skeleton along with the parts that genuinely
      wait. A search input the user could already be typing into, hidden behind gray bars, is known content
      held hostage by unknown content: the same fusion mistake as <a
        href='08-bundle-size-code-splitting.html#wholesale'>§8.2</a
      >, drawn in fallback form. Draw the boundary tightly around the data; everything outside it
      renders real and interactive immediately. (This is one of <a
        href='17-signs-doing-it-wrong.html'>§17</a
      >'s signs.)
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id='refetch'>11.5 Refetches show stale content, never fallbacks</h2>
  <p>
    <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='critical' /> A skeleton answers the question "what goes here?", a question that's only ever
    open <em>once</em>. On a refetch, the answer is already on screen. Replacing real content with a
    skeleton destroys information the user was looking at, resets their scroll and reading position,
    and shifts layout twice for zero benefit. The rule, in stale-while-revalidate terms:
    <strong
      >existing content is never replaced while new content loads; loading is only ever
      <em>indicated</em>, alongside the stale content.</strong
    >
  </p>

