  <p>The tools, by situation:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>URL-driven server UI (the default case)</strong>: <em>transitions</em>. The App Router
      performs navigations inside <code>startTransition</code>, so when a filter changes the URL,
      the already-revealed table stays on screen while the server re-renders with the new <code
        >searchParams</code
      >; the Suspense boundary does
      <em>not</em> revert to its fallback. Wrap the <code>router.replace()</code> in your own <code
        >useTransition</code
      > and <code>isPending</code> is your indicator. Assembled end to end in <a
        href='#example-url-transition'>the first example below</a
      >.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Client-fetched data</strong>: SWR / React Query already behave this way:
      <code>data</code> keeps its previous value while <code>isValidating</code>/<code
        >isFetching</code
      >
      is true (use <code>keepPreviousData</code> when the key changes). Bind your indicator to <code
        >isValidating</code
      >; never gate the content on it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Client-rendered results of a fast-changing value</strong>:
      <code>useDeferredValue</code>. When the value drives client-side rendering or client-fetched
      queries (an autocomplete, search-as-you-type against the client cache), render against the
      deferred value; <code>value !== deferredValue</code> drives the dimmed opacity. Same presentation
      rule, client-side mechanism. Shown in
      <a href='#example-deferred'>the second example below</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id='example-url-transition'>
    Example: URL-driven server UI, kept stale through a transition
  </h3>
  <p>
    The first bullet, the default case, assembled end to end. Notice it's the whole guide in one
    example: the URL is the state (<a href='09-server-resolvable-state.html'>§9</a>), the input and
    the dim-wrapper are client leaves (<a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html'>§5</a>), the rows
    are a server component fetching by <code>searchParams</code>, and the fallback appears exactly
    once:
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">app/reports/page.tsx - server: the URL is the input</span><span class="tok-kw">export default async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">Page</span>({ searchParams }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { q = <span class="tok-str">""</span> } = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> searchParams;
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    <span class="tok-com">// One shared, thin client provider gives the input and the table a single isPending.</span>
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">QueryTransitionProvider</span>&gt;
      <span class="tok-com">{/* This boundary is required because SearchInput reads the URL with
          useSearchParams (§5.6). It is necessary so that: (1) the static shell
          can prerender and stream without knowing the request's search params;
          (2) the client-side-rendering bailout stays scoped to this leaf instead
          of climbing to the nearest ancestor boundary and taking the whole page
          with it; and (3) the build passes under cacheComponents/PPR, which
          requires every request-time read to sit inside a Suspense boundary. */}</span>
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span> <span class="tok-prop">fallback</span>={&lt;<span class="tok-fn">SearchInputSkeleton</span> /&gt;}&gt;
        &lt;<span class="tok-fn">SearchInput</span> /&gt;
      &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span>&gt;
      <span class="tok-com">{/* The known container reserves the table's space (§10). */}</span>
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">TableContainer</span>&gt;
        <span class="tok-com">{/* A client shell that dims; the server-rendered rows pass through as children. */}</span>
        &lt;<span class="tok-fn">StaleContent</span>&gt;
          <span class="tok-com">{/* The fallback appears exactly once, on first load. */}</span>
          &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span> <span class="tok-prop">fallback</span>={&lt;<span class="tok-fn">TableSkeleton</span> /&gt;}&gt;
            <span class="tok-com">{/* A server component that fetches based on the query in the URL. */}</span>
            &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ReportRows</span> <span class="tok-prop">query</span>={q} /&gt;
          &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span>&gt;
        &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">StaleContent</span>&gt;
      &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">TableContainer</span>&gt;
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">QueryTransitionProvider</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
  </Snippet>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">ReportRows.tsx - server: only ever renders finished knowledge</span><span class="tok-kw">export async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ReportRows</span>({ query }) {
  <span class="tok-com">// The data layer call is cached and deduped (§15).</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> rows = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">getReportRows</span>(query);

  <span class="tok-com">/* The await above has finished by the time this renders, so "empty" here can
     only mean finished-and-empty; the unknown case cannot reach this component (§11.6). */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">if</span> (rows.length === <span class="tok-str">0</span>) <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;<span class="tok-fn">EmptyState</span> <span class="tok-prop">query</span>={query} /&gt;;

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Rows</span> <span class="tok-prop">rows</span>={rows} /&gt;;
}</pre>
  </Snippet>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">QueryTransition.tsx - client: one transition, shared by the leaves</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">const</span> Ctx = <span class="tok-fn">createContext</span>({ isPending: <span class="tok-kw">false</span>, update: (fn: () =&gt; <span class="tok-kw">void</span>) =&gt; {} });
<span class="tok-kw">export const</span> <span class="tok-fn">useQueryTransition</span> = () =&gt; <span class="tok-fn">use</span>(Ctx);

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">QueryTransitionProvider</span>({ children }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [isPending, startTransition] = <span class="tok-fn">useTransition</span>();
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;Ctx <span class="tok-prop">value</span>={{ isPending, update: startTransition }}&gt;{children}&lt;/Ctx&gt;;
}</pre>
  </Snippet>

  <Compare>
    <CompareCol kind='good' label='✓ SearchInput: client leaf, owns the URL'>
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-good'
          is:raw><span class="filename">SearchInput.tsx</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">SearchInput</span>() {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> params = <span class="tok-fn">useSearchParams</span>();
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> router = <span class="tok-fn">useRouter</span>();
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { isPending, update } = <span class="tok-fn">useQueryTransition</span>();

  <span class="tok-com">/* The change handler is debounced upstream;
     each accepted change rewrites the URL inside
     the shared transition, which keeps the stale
     rows visible. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Input</span>
      <span class="tok-prop">defaultValue</span>={params.<span class="tok-fn">get</span>(<span class="tok-str">"q"</span>) ?? <span class="tok-str">""</span>}
      <span class="tok-prop">isLoading</span>={isPending}
      <span class="tok-prop">onValueChange</span>={(q) =&gt;
        <span class="tok-fn">update</span>(() =&gt; {
          <span class="tok-kw">const</span> next = <span class="tok-fn">withParam</span>(params, <span class="tok-str">"q"</span>, q);
          router.<span class="tok-fn">replace</span>(<span class="tok-str">`?${</span>next<span class="tok-str">}`</span>);
        })}
    /&gt;
  );
}</pre>
      </Snippet>
    </CompareCol>
    <CompareCol kind='good' label='✓ StaleContent: client shell, dims what passes through'>
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-good'
          is:raw><span class="filename">StaleContent.tsx</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">StaleContent</span>({ children }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { isPending } = <span class="tok-fn">useQueryTransition</span>();

  <span class="tok-com">/* The children are the server-rendered rows,
     passing through the boundary (§5.4). This
     shell only indicates loading; it never
     replaces them. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;div <span class="tok-prop">style</span>={{ opacity: isPending ? <span class="tok-str">0.6</span> : <span class="tok-str">1</span> }}&gt;
      {children}
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}</pre>
      </Snippet>
    </CompareCol>
  </Compare>

  <p>
    The flow on a filter change: the input calls <code>router.replace()</code> inside the shared transition
    → the server re-renders <code>ReportRows</code> with the new query → because it's a transition and
    the boundary has already revealed,
    <strong>the stale rows never leave the screen</strong>; they just dim until the fresh server
    render streams in and swaps. The skeleton appeared exactly once, at first load. And note what <code
      >ReportRows</code
    > gets for free by being a server component: it only ever runs when the data is <em>finished</em
    >, so the unknown-vs-empty question (<a href='#orthogonal'>§11.6</a>) collapses: <code
      >undefined</code
    > can't reach it, and the empty check is honestly just "finished and empty." One caveat to keep it
    honest: don't put a
    <code>key</code> tied to the query on the Suspense boundary: a remounted boundary is "new content"
    and <em>will</em> re-show its fallback.
  </p>

  <h3 id='example-deferred'>Example: client-rendered results of a fast-changing value</h3>
  <p>
    The third bullet. Here nothing touches the server or the URL: the data is already on the client,
    and the fast-changing value is each keystroke. <code>useDeferredValue</code>
    gives the same stale-content behavior with a purely client mechanism: the input re-renders instantly
    with the fresh value, while the expensive results below are allowed to lag one render behind and dim
    until they catch up:
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">ProgramFilter.tsx - client: search-as-you-type over client data</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramFilter</span>({ programs }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [query, setQuery] = <span class="tok-fn">useState</span>(<span class="tok-str">""</span>);

  <span class="tok-com">/* deferredQuery lags behind query while React re-renders the heavy list.
     The input stays instantly responsive; the results are allowed to be one
     keystroke stale. */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> deferredQuery = <span class="tok-fn">useDeferredValue</span>(query);
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> isStale = query !== deferredQuery;

  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> matches = <span class="tok-fn">useMemo</span>(
    () =&gt; programs.<span class="tok-fn">filter</span>((p) =&gt; p.name.<span class="tok-fn">toLowerCase</span>().<span class="tok-fn">includes</span>(deferredQuery.<span class="tok-fn">toLowerCase</span>())),
    [programs, deferredQuery]
  );

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Input</span> <span class="tok-prop">value</span>={query} <span class="tok-prop">onValueChange</span>={setQuery} /&gt;

      <span class="tok-com">{/* Same presentation rule as the server case: stale results stay
          visible and dim; they are never swapped for a spinner. */}</span>
      &lt;div <span class="tok-prop">style</span>={{ opacity: isStale ? <span class="tok-str">0.6</span> : <span class="tok-str">1</span> }}&gt;
        &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramList</span> <span class="tok-prop">programs</span>={matches} /&gt;
      &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/&gt;
  );
}</pre>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    Two mechanisms, one rule. The transition example indicates with
    <code>isPending</code>; this one indicates with <code>query !== deferredQuery</code>. In both,
    the existing results never leave the screen, and nothing below the input ever collapses into a
    spinner. (The middle bullet, client-fetched data, needs no example of its own: SWR and React
    Query give you this behavior out of the box; just bind your indicator to <code
      >isValidating</code
    > and never gate content on it.)
  </p>

  <h2 id='orthogonal'>11.6 Loading, data, and empty are three separate questions</h2>
  <p>
    The deepest of the loading-state mistakes is structural: the
    <code>if (isLoading) … else if (!data) … else …</code> chain. It welds three independent questions
    into one sequence, and every branch is wrong in some state:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>"Is a request in flight?"</strong> A <em>status</em>, true on first load and on every
      refetch. It decorates the UI (indicator, dimming); it must never decide
      <em>which</em> UI renders; that's how refetches turn into skeletons.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>"Do we have data?"</strong> A question about <em>knowledge</em>:
      <code>undefined</code> means <em>we don't know yet</em> (no request has finished). It's answered
      by the presence of a value, not by the loading flag.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>"Is the data empty?"</strong> Only askable <em>after</em> we have knowledge: the request
      finished and returned nothing. Empty state visibility has exactly one condition: <strong
        >finished, and empty.</strong
      > It has nothing to do with loading.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This is also why the data layer must never default missing data to an empty array.
    <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='critical' />
    <code>data ?? []</code> feels like defensive convenience, but it
    <em>erases the distinction between "unknown" and "known-empty"</em>; downstream code can no
    longer tell whether to show a skeleton or an empty state, and the classic bug appears: an "No
    results" flash on every first load, before the data arrives.
    <code>undefined</code> is information. Preserve it end to end, and let the leaf that renders decide.
  </p>

