  <h3 id='one-skeleton'>One container, one skeleton, ever</h3>
  <p>
    The table reads row by row; watched as one container over its lifetime, it collapses to a single
    rule: <strong
      >the skeleton appears exactly once, on first load, while the answer is unknown.</strong
    > The moment the first response lands, the container has something honest to show forever after: rows
    if there are rows, a real empty state if there are none. Every fetch from then on (revalidation, filter
    change, a mutation's refresh) <em>decorates</em> that content instead of replacing it: same UI, dimmed
    or badged while the request is in flight. And the treatment does not depend on what the content is.
    A known-empty list being refreshed keeps its empty state and gains the indicator, exactly like a populated
    list keeps its rows; the skeleton does not get to come back just because the container happens to
    be empty.
  </p>

