  <h2 id='transitions'>11.7 Transitions</h2>
  <p>
    Every state update in React is <em>urgent</em> by default: React drops what it is doing and re-renders
    to reflect it as fast as it can. <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='very' />
    <code>useTransition</code> (and its standalone form <code>startTransition</code>) marks an
    update as
    <strong>non-urgent</strong>: React prepares the resulting UI <em>in the background</em>
    while the current one stays visible and interactive, and if a genuinely urgent update (a keystroke)
    lands mid-render, React abandons the background work, handles the keystroke, and restarts. One flag,
    and three behaviors fall out of it, each a distinct tool.
  </p>

  <h3 id='transitions-stale'>Transitions keep the old UI alive</h3>
  <p>
    The callout in <a href='#mechanism'>§11.1</a> noted that a boundary already showing content will not
    fall back to its skeleton when an update inside it suspends during a transition. That behavior is
    how you get updates that don't flash, and it is the same doctrine as <a href='#refetch'>§11.5</a
    >, one level up: don't blank what you already have just because something newer is on the way.
    This is the reason writes to router-owned state, a navigation or a <code>searchParams</code> update
    (<a href='09-server-resolvable-state.html#url-state'>§9.2</a>), belong in a transition: the
    current page stays live while the next view prepares, and the <code>isPending</code> flag from <code
      >useTransition</code
    > shows progress
    <em>alongside</em> the old content instead of replacing it.
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">SortSelect.tsx - client leaf: a router write that keeps the table alive</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">SortSelect</span>({ current }: { current: string }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> router = <span class="tok-fn">useRouter</span>();
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [isPending, startTransition] = <span class="tok-fn">useTransition</span>();

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Select</span>
      <span class="tok-prop">value</span>={current}
      <span class="tok-prop">disabled</span>={isPending}
      <span class="tok-prop">onChange</span>={(sort) =&gt;
        <span class="tok-fn">startTransition</span>(() =&gt; {
          <span class="tok-com">/* A router write: the server re-renders the page.
             Inside a transition, the table's boundary keeps
             its revealed rows instead of falling back. */</span>
          router.<span class="tok-fn">push</span>(<span class="tok-str">`/programs?sort=${sort}`</span>);
        })
      }
    /&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      While the server prepares the new rows, <code>isPending</code> is true: dim the table, disable the
      select, badge the header; the old UI never blanks. The end-to-end version of this pattern is the
      <a href='#example-url-transition'>§11.5 example</a>.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <h3 id='transitions-defer'>Transitions defer expensive work</h3>
  <p>
    The second use has nothing to do with data: a state update whose <em>re-render</em> is expensive.
    Re-filtering a large list, re-laying-out a chart, switching a heavy tab: wrap the update in <code
      >startTransition</code
    > and the typing, clicking, and scrolling stay responsive while the heavy render runs at low priority,
    restarted rather than blocking whenever the user does something urgent. Two caveats from the React
    docs are worth knowing. A transition cannot drive a controlled text input: the input's own value is
    urgent by definition (the user must see their keystroke), so the transition carries the expensive
    <em>derived</em> state and the input updates normally. And when the value whose update you would defer
    is not yours to wrap (it arrives through props or a store),
    <code>useDeferredValue</code> is the same mechanism applied to a value instead of an update: the type-to-filter
    example in <a href='#example-deferred'>§11.5</a>.
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">ProgramSearch.tsx - client: urgent keystroke, non-urgent filter</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramSearch</span>({ all }: { all: <span class="tok-fn">Program</span>[] }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [query, setQuery] = <span class="tok-fn">useState</span>(<span class="tok-str">""</span>);
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [results, setResults] = <span class="tok-fn">useState</span>(all);
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [isPending, startTransition] = <span class="tok-fn">useTransition</span>();

  <span class="tok-kw">function</span> <span class="tok-fn">onInput</span>(next: string) {
    <span class="tok-com">// Urgent: the keystroke must paint immediately.</span>
    <span class="tok-fn">setQuery</span>(next);
    <span class="tok-fn">startTransition</span>(() =&gt; {
      <span class="tok-com">// Non-urgent: rendered in the background, restarted
      // if another keystroke lands mid-render.</span>
      <span class="tok-fn">setResults</span>(<span class="tok-fn">filterPrograms</span>(all, next));
    });
  }

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Input</span> <span class="tok-prop">value</span>={query} <span class="tok-prop">onChange</span>={onInput} /&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ResultsList</span> <span class="tok-prop">items</span>={results} <span class="tok-prop">dimmed</span>={isPending} /&gt;
    &lt;/&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      Two updates from one keystroke. The input's own value is the urgent one and paints at once;
      the expensive re-filter of <code>results</code> runs at low priority and never blocks typing.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <h3 id='transitions-async'>Async transitions: actions and pending state</h3>
  <p>
    React 19 extended transitions to <em>async</em> functions, which the docs call
    <strong>actions</strong>: <code>startTransition(async () =&gt; &#123; … &#125;)</code>.
    <code>isPending</code> flips to true at the first call and stays true until every update the action
    scheduled has landed, which is exactly the lifecycle a submit button needs; this is the machinery
    behind firing a server action from a leaf (<a
      href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#self-contained'>§5.7</a
    >), where <code>isPending</code> drives the button and the result object carries the failure (<a
      href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#server-actions'>§5.8</a
    >). Errors thrown inside an action surface to the nearest error boundary, and optimistic UI (<code
      >useOptimistic</code
    >, <a href='09-server-resolvable-state.html#url-state'>§9.2</a>) reverts automatically when the
    action fails. One sharp edge from the docs: after an <code>await</code>, further state updates
    inside the action must be wrapped in
    <code>startTransition</code> again, or they lose their transition marking.
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">ArchiveButton.tsx - client leaf: an async action</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ArchiveButton</span>({ programId }: { programId: number }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> [isPending, startTransition] = <span class="tok-fn">useTransition</span>();

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Button</span>
      <span class="tok-prop">disabled</span>={isPending}
      <span class="tok-prop">onClick</span>={() =&gt;
        <span class="tok-fn">startTransition</span>(<span class="tok-kw">async</span> () =&gt; {
          <span class="tok-com">/* The action: awaited inside the transition, so
             isPending covers the server round trip AND the
             re-render its revalidation triggers. */</span>
          <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { error } = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">archiveProgram</span>(programId);
          <span class="tok-kw">if</span> (error) toast.<span class="tok-fn">error</span>(error);
        })
      }
    &gt;
      {isPending ? <span class="tok-str">"Archiving…"</span> : <span class="tok-str">"Archive"</span>}
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Button</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      The same machinery as the publish button in
      <a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#self-contained'>§5.7</a>:
      <code>isPending</code> flips at the first line and holds until every update the action scheduled
      has landed, so the button cannot double-fire and the label is honest.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    The API reference for <code>useTransition</code> and <code>useDeferredValue</code> is in
    <a href='19-new-primitives.html#use-transition'>§19.3</a>; the caveat list lives in the
    <a href='https://react.dev/reference/react/startTransition' target='_blank' rel='noopener'
      >React docs</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2 id='rules'>The rules</h2>
  <table class='tbl'>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Rule</th>
        <th>Scope</th>
        <th>Importance</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Components <strong>never define their own loading states</strong>; boundaries above them
          do.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Fallbacks live <strong>inside known containers</strong>; neither fallback nor suspended
          component includes the container.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Skeletons are <strong>scoped per component, colocated with it</strong>, never page-level.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='very' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Refetches never replace content:
          <strong>stale stays visible, loading is indicated</strong>
          (<code>isValidating</code>, <code>useDeferredValue</code>,
          <code>isPending</code>).
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Loading status, data presence, and emptiness are
          <strong>decided independently</strong>: no if/else chains across them.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          The data layer never defaults <code>undefined</code> to <code>[]</code>;
          <strong>unknown and known-empty are different facts</strong>.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Use <strong>granular Suspense boundaries per data region</strong>;
          <code>loading.tsx</code> renders scaffold, not a page skeleton.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='next' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='very' /></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

